"use strict";(self.webpackChunkdatabricks_monolith=self.webpackChunkdatabricks_monolith||[]).push([[99740],{999740:e=>{e.exports="# How to configure datasource health monitoring\n\nHealth monitoring analyzes the data volume in the bronze table on a schedule and raises operational alerts when the volume deviates anomalously from historical baselines. It runs as a separate Databricks Job from the main ingestion pipeline.\n\n## When to suggest it\n\n- User asks about monitoring data volume or data freshness.\n- User asks why there are no alerts when data stops arriving.\n- User asks how to get notified if their bronze table stops receiving data.\n- User asks to enable or configure health monitoring.\n\n## Enabling health monitoring\n\nHealth monitoring is controlled by the `health` key in the datasource spec. Set `enabled: true` to enable it:\n\n```yaml\nspec:\n  health:\n    enabled: true\n```\n\nAll other fields are optional and have sensible defaults. A minimal enabled config is sufficient for most datasources.\n\n## All configurable fields\n\n```yaml\nspec:\n  health:\n    enabled: true # Whether health monitoring is active\n    mode: hour-of-week # Comparison mode (see below)\n    schedule: '0 0 * * * ? *' # Quartz cron, defaults to every hour on the hour\n    lagIntervalHours: 1 # Hours to wait before analyzing (accounts for ingestion lag)\n    baselineInterval: 30d # How far back to look for baseline data (e.g., 7d, 14d, 30d)\n    threshold: 3.0 # Alert when volume deviates by this many standard deviations\n    ignorePositiveExcursions: false # true = only alert on LOW volume (not high)\n    analyzeFrom: '2024-01-01' # Earliest date to include in analysis (YYYY-MM-DD)\n```\n\n## Comparison modes\n\n| Mode           | Behavior                                                                                                 | Best for                                     |\n| -------------- | -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- | -------------------------------------------- |\n| `hour-of-week` | Compares each hour to the same hour on the same weekday (e.g., Monday 3 PM vs. prior Monday 3 PM values) | Data with weekly patterns (default)          |\n| `hour-of-day`  | Compares each hour to the same hour across all days                                                      | Data with consistent daily patterns          |\n| `hourly`       | Compares each hour to any recent hours in the baseline window                                            | General-purpose; no weekly/daily assumptions |\n\n## Common configurations\n\n### Enable with defaults (recommended starting point)\n\n```yaml\nspec:\n  health:\n    enabled: true\n```\n\n### Tighter alerting — alert on 2σ deviation instead of 3σ\n\n```yaml\nspec:\n  health:\n    enabled: true\n    threshold: 2.0\n```\n\n### Only alert on data going missing (not on spikes)\n\n```yaml\nspec:\n  health:\n    enabled: true\n    ignorePositiveExcursions: true\n```\n\n### Weekly pattern with longer baseline\n\n```yaml\nspec:\n  health:\n    enabled: true\n    mode: hour-of-week\n    baselineInterval: 60d\n```\n\n### Disable health monitoring\n\n```yaml\nspec:\n  health:\n    enabled: false\n```\n\n## Updating via the tool\n\nUse `pageUpdateLakewatchDatasourceSpec` with a partial YAML containing only the `spec.health` path. Always present a summary before calling the tool.\n\nExample partial YAML to enable with default settings:\n\n```yaml\nspec:\n  health:\n    enabled: true\n```\n\nExample partial YAML to disable:\n\n```yaml\nspec:\n  health:\n    enabled: false\n```\n"}}]);
